    {
      "abstract": "This article presents a model predictive control (MPC) framework for repositioning floating offshore wind turbines (FOWTs), with an explicit focus on managing transient system dynamics. Repositioning turbines within a wind farm is effective in mitigating wake effects and improving wind farm power production, but it introduces transient dynamics that, if not properly controlled, can lead to platform instability and degrade power quality. Prior studies focus on steady-state performance and overlook these crucial dynamic effects. The proposed MPC approach balances power capture, platform oscillation suppression, actuator usage, and repositioning speed to ensure safe and efficient turbine movement. A medium-fidelity simulation environment is used to implement and evaluate the controller under a variety of turbulent wind and irregular wave conditions. Results show that the proposed controller effectively manages dynamic tradeoffs during repositioning and improves control performance compared to conventional strategies. This work lays the groundwork for future research in coordinated, transient-aware repositioning control for floating offshore wind farms (FOWFs).",
